      "raw_text": "Inrecentyears,theproliferationofWiFi-connected devices and related research has led to novel techniques of Moving Occupant utilizing WiFi as sensors, i.e., capturing human movements Stationary Occupant through channel state information (CSI) perturbations. While AP this enables passive occupant sensing, it also introduces privacy Client Walk Sit risks from leaked WiFi signals that attackers can intercept, leading to threats like occupancy detection, critical in scenarios such as burglaries or stalking. We propose LeakyBeam, a novel and improved occupancy detection attack that leverages a new Victim Residence side channel from WiFi CSI, namely beamforming feedback information (BFI). BFI retains victim's movement information, Leaked BFI Packets Attacker with evenwhentransmittedthroughwalls,andiseasilycapturedsince (Plaintext) Sniffer BFI packets are unencrypted, making them a rich source of privacy-sensitive information. Furthermore, we also introduce Fig. 1: Figure depicts an attack scenario using LeakyBeam, a defense mechanism that obfuscates BFI packets, requiring where an attacker uses a distant, passive sniffer to capture minimal hardware changes. We demonstrate LeakyBeam's ef- BFIpacketsfromthevictim'scommercialWiFiclientdevices, fectiveness through a comprehensive real-world evaluation at a leaked outside the residence, to infer the occupancy state by distance of 20 meters, achieving true positive and negative rates identifying both moving and stationary occupants. of 82.7% and 96.7%, respectively.",
